Store uploads in Supabase so they survive deploys

The free Render plan has an ephemeral filesystem, so every deploy erased the
user's uploads (and, with SQLite, the whole library). This adds an optional
Supabase backend:

- app/objectstore.py wraps the Supabase Storage REST API (create bucket, put,
  delete, public URL). No SDK needed.
- Uploads are written to the local disk for the life of the process *and* copied
  to the bucket. Only a successful copy sets storage_backend="supabase".
- /api/stream and /tracks/{id}/artwork 302-redirect to the public bucket URL for
  those tracks, which keeps this small instance out of the data path and still
  gives Range support (Supabase serves it).
- Deleting a track also deletes both objects.
- DATABASE_URL now normalises to postgresql+psycopg, and psycopg[binary] is a
  dependency, so metadata can live in Supabase Postgres instead of the ephemeral
  SQLite file.
- render.yaml declares DATABASE_URL / SUPABASE_URL / SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY with
  sync: false, so Render prompts for them rather than storing secrets in the
  public repository.
- GET /api/storage/status reports whether uploads are actually durable, and the
  app's Settings screen shows it, so this is visible instead of assumed.

Verified locally with storage disabled (health reports files:"local", full
upload/stream/artwork/delete cycle passes) and the Postgres DDL compiles for the
psycopg dialect without a server.
